In this case, the second determinant factor is zero, and hence
$WQ\widetilde A-I$ is singular. This conclusion only uses the component
$\mbox{${\mathcal V}$}_2=\{2,4\}$; no assumption about the component $\mbox{${\mathcal V}$}_1=\{1,3\}$ is needed.

\item[(ii)] Let $q_2=q_4=1$, assume  $q_1,q_3\notin\{0,1\}$ (that is,  $1$ and $3$ are not in $\mbox{${\mathcal P}$}\cup\mbox{${\mathcal D}$}$), and also let
$\mu_{12}=\mu_{34}=1$. 
